1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:05,496
On Thursday, a developer on the marketplace team turns on a debug log, and forgets it.

2
00:00:05,546 --> 00:00:11,952
For two days, the log writes the full marketplace tokens of its customers, and Aziz is one of them.

3
00:00:12,002 --> 00:00:18,360
Someone with access to the logs copies his token, and sends it to the backend of the mobile bank, to move money.

4
00:00:18,410 --> 00:00:22,368
The token is real, Keycloak signed it, and it has not expired.

5
00:00:22,418 --> 00:00:26,336
So why does the mobile bank refuse it?

6
00:00:26,386 --> 00:00:32,744
In Lesson 4, one installation sat inside the perimeter of the bank, with one Keycloak for all apps.

7
00:00:32,794 --> 00:00:36,488
This lesson shows how one Keycloak keeps the apps apart.

8
00:00:36,538 --> 00:00:38,360
Start with the realm.

9
00:00:38,410 --> 00:00:43,736
A realm is one space in Keycloak, with its own users, keys and settings.

10
00:00:43,786 --> 00:00:48,320
heeyID has one realm for the installation, so one bank has one realm.

11
00:00:48,370 --> 00:00:57,824
Inside the realm, each app of the bank is one client: the mobile bank, the marketplace app, the marketplace web profile and the credit service.

12
00:00:57,874 --> 00:01:01,832
When Keycloak issues a token, it writes two names into it.

13
00:01:01,882 --> 00:01:06,248
The first is the audience, aud: who the token is for.

14
00:01:06,298 --> 00:01:11,120
The second is the authorised party, azp: which app asked for it.

15
00:01:11,170 --> 00:01:13,640
In heeyID, both name the same app.

16
00:01:13,690 --> 00:01:20,096
So the token of Aziz says, in a signed field: marketplace, and only marketplace.

17
00:01:20,146 --> 00:01:21,968
Now the rule.

18
00:01:22,018 --> 00:01:25,880
The backend of each app accepts only the tokens of its own app.

19
00:01:25,930 --> 00:01:32,696
The mobile bank backend reads the audience and the authorised party, sees the marketplace, and refuses.

20
00:01:32,746 --> 00:01:37,280
The answer is a fixed error: session of a different app, log in again.

21
00:01:37,330 --> 00:01:40,784
The stolen token opens nothing but the marketplace.

22
00:01:40,834 --> 00:01:43,280
heeyID checks the same rule at its own door.

23
00:01:43,330 --> 00:01:47,984
Its request guard looks at the token before any module sees the request.

24
00:01:48,034 --> 00:01:52,544
It checks that the token is for this app, by the audience and the authorised party.

25
00:01:52,594 --> 00:01:56,888
Then it checks the device binding: the token belongs to one device record.

26
00:01:56,938 --> 00:02:01,304
Why not something simpler, like a header that says the app is the mobile bank?

27
00:02:01,354 --> 00:02:03,176
Because anyone can write a header.

28
00:02:03,226 --> 00:02:10,664
I think this is the main idea of the lesson: a header says what a caller wants to be, and a signed token says what it is.

29
00:02:10,714 --> 00:02:15,464
heeyID takes the app from the verified token, never from a header or a path.

30
00:02:15,514 --> 00:02:20,912
There is one moment with no token: the start of a registration, or of a login.

31
00:02:20,962 --> 00:02:27,008
Then the app comes from the app identifier of the build, and heeyID checks it against its catalogue of apps.

32
00:02:27,058 --> 00:02:29,744
The same rule goes deeper than the door.

33
00:02:29,794 --> 00:02:32,888
Each session and each app account carries its app.

34
00:02:32,938 --> 00:02:39,848
Each query of app data filters by the app of the token, and the repository layer adds that filter, not the caller.

35
00:02:39,898 --> 00:02:45,512
So even a bug in a module cannot read the marketplace account through a mobile bank session.

36
00:02:45,562 --> 00:02:50,312
Cache keys with app data carry the app, and each event carries the app.

37
00:02:50,362 --> 00:02:59,120
When one app needs data of another app account, it asks through a request that the customer answers, for example the address-sharing request in the master app.

38
00:02:59,170 --> 00:03:00,992
Back to the leak.

39
00:03:01,042 --> 00:03:02,768
What does the person with the logs get?

40
00:03:02,818 --> 00:03:05,696
The marketplace, for Aziz, for a short time.

41
00:03:05,746 --> 00:03:09,752
The access token lives only for a short, configured time.

42
00:03:09,802 --> 00:03:17,336
The refresh token rotates at each renewal, and the platform detects a refresh token that comes back a second time.

43
00:03:17,386 --> 00:03:20,480
And the session is bound to the device of Aziz.

44
00:03:20,530 --> 00:03:25,808
That is a real incident for the marketplace team, but it is not a way to the money of Aziz.

45
00:03:25,858 --> 00:03:27,488
How do we know it stays this way?

46
00:03:27,538 --> 00:03:34,040
At each release, the acceptance tests send a marketplace token to the mobile bank backend, and expect that error.

47
00:03:34,090 --> 00:03:36,992
If a release forgets the check, the release stops.

48
00:03:37,042 --> 00:03:39,056
One more thing comes with one realm.

49
00:03:39,106 --> 00:03:45,368
A new app of the bank is one more client and one more row in the app catalogue, not a new installation.

50
00:03:45,418 --> 00:03:47,744
Each app gets its own required level.

51
00:03:47,794 --> 00:03:52,400
The credit service can need level two, while another app needs only level one.

52
00:03:52,450 --> 00:03:56,912
To recap: one realm for the installation, and one client for each app.

53
00:03:56,962 --> 00:04:02,288
Each token names its app two times, as the audience and the authorised party.

54
00:04:02,338 --> 00:04:08,648
Each backend accepts only its own tokens, and heeyID checks the same at its door and in every query.

55
00:04:08,698 --> 00:04:11,744
So the leaked token stays in the marketplace.

56
00:04:11,794 --> 00:04:17,912
But look at the values in this lesson: the token lifetime, the required level of each app, the limits.

57
00:04:17,962 --> 00:04:20,696
Who sets those values, and where do they live?

58
00:04:20,746 --> 00:04:22,640
Next: configuration.
